  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nFrom Monday night through next weekend, the main concern will \ncontinue to center around the potential for significant \nprecipitation late this week. Models continue to converge around a\ngeneral solution bringing a significant upper level trough \nthrough the southern plains this week after absorbing and picking \nup moisture from the remnants of what is now Hurricane Polo. The \nremnants of Polo are expected to be absorbed into the upper level \ntrough Monday night into Tuesday morning from northwest Mexico \nwhen the upper trough is still making its way east into Arizona. \nWe will still be hot Tuesday, with the best chance for \nprecipitation still west of us as well. By Wednesday afternoon, \nthe GFS places the trough axis in eastern New Mexico, with the \nECMWF a tad slower. The speed of the upper trough will have a \nsignificant effect on when and where the heaviest precipitation \ntakes place, especially for our western counties. At this time, it\nappears that a surface Pacific cold frontal boundary will move \ninto our western counties Wednesday afternoon and evening. At the \nsame time, both the GFS and ECM suggest that the remnant vorticity\nmax from Polo could be wrapping into the upper level trough over \nwest Texas. This could potentially enhance precipitation chances \nand amounts across west and west- central Texas, especially as was\nmentioned in the previous discussion, precipitable waters will be\napproach 2.5 inches in our area during this time period. However,\nthe timing will be critical, and if the system is faster than \ncurrently anticipated, much of our area will see significantly \nless rainfall, especially west of an Abilene to Sonora line. There\nis more confidence in heavier rainfall amounts east of that same \nline. In either case, the potential is there for the entire area \nto have concerns about heavy rainfall, but confidence is higher in\nareas east of an Abilene to Sonora line. \n\nExpect most of the area to see precipitation ending by Thursday \nnight. However, if the upper level trough slows significantly, \nthere will be additional precipitation for areas east of a Coleman\nto Sonora line into Friday. Temperatures will also finally cool \noff, with highs expected to cool from the 90s on Tuesday into the \n80s by Thursday, and potentially into the 70s from Friday into the\nweekend.",
